: the 1 at (23,13) in pair 2. It is row-aligned with block 3 at a gap of 6 and stays where it is. The 1 at (15,18) in the same pair is a mirror image of it. It is row-aligned with block 2 at a gap of 6, and it is also as close to block 3 under the Chebyshev metric (the larger of the row and column distance). Yet it moves.\n\nSo the Chebyshev idea was not wrong. What broke my second program was how it handled ties: it froze every tied cell, which wrongly froze (15,18).\n\nA tie-break that fits both cases is reading order. Blocks are numbered in reading order (top to bottom, then left to right), and a tie goes to the earlier one:\n- **(15,18):** tie between block 2 and block 3. Block 2 wins. It is aligned with the cell, so the cell slides left to (15,12).\n- **(23,13):** tie between block 2 and block 3. Block 2 wins again, but it does not share a row or column with the cell. So the cell stays.\n\n**Final rule:**\n- The 2×2 blocks never change.\n- Each 1 picks its nearest block by Chebyshev distance. On a tie it picks the first such block in reading order.\n- If that block shares a row or column with the 1, the 1 slides along that line until it touches the block. Otherwise the 1 stays where it is.\n\nPairs 1 and 3 contain no ties, so this rule makes the same choices there as the earlier passing versions. In pair 2, the reading-order tie-break fixes exactly the two contested cells, (15,18) and (23,13).\n\n**On the test input:**\n- Block 0 (rows 5–6, cols 5–6) attracts three 1s:\n  - (3,5) moves to (4,5).\n  - (10,5) moves to (7,5).\n  - (6,10) moves to (6,7).\n- Block 1 (rows 16–17, cols 9–10) attracts three 1s:\n  - (13,9) moves to (15,9).\n  - (17,2) moves to (17,8).\n  - (17,13) moves to (17,11). It is nearer to block 1 (distance 3) than to block 2 (distance 4).\n- Block 2 (rows 16–17, cols 17–18) attracts three 1s:\n  - (16,14) moves to (16,16).\n  - (11,18) moves to (15,18).\n  - (17,22) moves to (17,19).\n- Block 3 (rows 25–26, cols 22–23) attracts one 1:\n  - (26,14) moves to (26,21).\n- All other 1s have no block in their row or column, so they stay.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    blocks = [o for o in objs if o[\"size\"] > 1]\n    blocks.sort(key=lambda o: (o[\"box\"][0], o[\"box\"][1]))  # reading order\n    ones = [o[\"cells\"][0] for o in objs if o[\"size\"] == 1]\n\n    def cheb(y, x, box):\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = box\n        dy = max(y0 - y, 0, y - y1)\n        dx = max(x0 - x, 0, x - x1)\n        return max(dy, dx)\n\n    moves = {}\n    for (y, x) in ones:\n        if not blocks:\n            break\n        ds = [cheb(y, x, b[\"box\"]) for b in blocks]\n        i = ds.index(min(ds))  # ties -> first block in reading order\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = blocks[i][\"box\"]\n        dst = None\n        if y0 <= y <= y1:\n            dst = (y, x0 - 1) if x < x0 else (y, x1 + 1)\n        elif x0 <= x <= x1:\n            dst = (y0 - 1, x) if y < y0 else (y1 + 1, x)\n        if dst is None:\n            continue  # nearest block not in line: cell stays\n        moves.setdefault(i, []).append(((y, x), dst))\n\n    for i in sorted(moves):\n        for (s, d) in moves[i]:\n            out[s] = bg\n        for (s, d) in moves[i]:\n            out[d] = g[s]\n        emit(out, f\"Slid the {len(moves[i])} single cell(s) whose nearest block is block {i} \"\n                  f\"(box {blocks[i]['box']}) along their row/column until they touch it.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every single cell whose nearest block is in line with it now touches that block; others stay.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The 1 at (15,18) in the same pair is a mirror image of it."}],"movement":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is row-aligned with block 3 at a gap of 6 and stays where it is."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is row-aligned with block 2 at a gap of 6, and it is also as close to block 3 under the Chebyshev metric (the larger of the row and column distance)."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is aligned with the cell, so the cell slides left to (15,12)."}],"order":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"A tie-break that fits both cases is reading order."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Blocks are numbered in reading order (top to bottom, then left to right), and a tie goes to the earlier one:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"On a tie it picks the first such block in reading order."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"dcae1412848da50a0d37577a9cc7ef3cc2cee7638f9022dcf7adb99523935353","task_sha256":"e1d6efe2be0595e4418f9f4cc1f62c8d336e3769dad75b9293193e630ea5fd52","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
